# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is the default on Windows installs of Git, and without this
# file every shell script in the repo is checked out CRLF. That is not a
# cosmetic problem here, because these scripts do not stay on Windows:
#
#   * ops/deploy/install.sh is tarred to an Ubuntu instance by aws-demo.sh and
#     run there. Linux bash does not tolerate a CRLF shebang -- `#!/usr/bin/env
#     bash\r` fails as "bad interpreter: no such file or directory", naming a
#     binary that plainly exists;
#   * ops/postgres/init/*.sh are bind-mounted into the postgres container and
#     run by its entrypoint on first init. The same failure, except it happens
#     inside a container that then reports healthy while holding no roles;
#   * ops/keycloak/*.mjs and the Dockerfiles travel into images the same way.
#
# Git Bash happens to tolerate CRLF scripts, which is what makes this worth
# writing down: it works on the machine you test it on and fails on the machine
# it is for.
* text=auto

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Caddyfile*      text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers stay CRLF.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary

# Staff CSVs are read by the importer inside a Linux container; a stray CR
# ends up mid-record and the header parses short.
*.csv   text eol=lf
